      "class": "CYP17 androgen-biosynthesis inhibitor used with corticosteroid therapy",
      "description": "Abiraterone acetate is converted to abiraterone, which suppresses androgen production in the testes, adrenal glands, and prostate-tumor tissue. This matters because metastatic prostate cancers can continue using low concentrations of androgen even after testicular testosterone is suppressed. Blocking CYP17 lowers that remaining androgen signal, but it also diverts steroid precursors toward mineralocorticoids. The same mechanism that slows the cancer can therefore cause hypertension, hypokalemia, and fluid retention, which is why prednisone and close monitoring are part of therapy rather than optional add-ons.",
      "mechanism": "Abiraterone irreversibly inhibits CYP17A1, including 17-alpha-hydroxylase and C17,20-lyase activity. These reactions are required to make androgen precursors. Reduced androgen synthesis weakens androgen-receptor signaling in prostate-cancer cells. Upstream steroid precursors then accumulate and increase mineralocorticoid activity; glucocorticoid feedback also falls. Prednisone replaces part of that glucocorticoid signal, reduces ACTH drive, and limits mineralocorticoid excess. Sudden interruption of corticosteroid support during stress or infection can expose adrenocortical insufficiency.",
